minixml: parseatt: stop at the end of the buffer after '='

parseatt() steps past the '=' of an attribute and reads the next character
without checking for the end of the buffer, so a device description or SOAP
reply from a spoofed gateway that ends in '=' reads one byte past the heap
buffer.

Upstream commit 3cfb4fb78d5ac04ed0dadc8dd842fc9e448916db, made after
miniupnpc 2.3.3; only its code change, not the copyright header update.

--- a/src/minixml.c
+++ b/src/minixml.c
@@ -63,6 +63,9 @@
 				if(p->xml >= p->xmlend)
 					return -1;
 			}
+			/* p->xml points now to the character right after the '=' */
+			if(p->xml >= p->xmlend)
+				return -1;
 			while(IS_WHITE_SPACE(*p->xml))
 			{
 				p->xml++;
